From c09ad22d3a557b528eae9ebdcde77cff5f6da755 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:14:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: a page flip that never comes no longer stops
 Kodi on nvidia-drm

Seen once with an RTX 4080 SUPER, after native Dolby Vision output had
ended: the change back to the mode of the GUI took 6.2 s, nvidia-modeset
logged "Lost display notification (0:0x00000000); continuing", and no
flip came after it. Kodi waited for the flip of the frame before with
EGL_FOREVER_KHR, so it stopped there for good: no picture on the TV, no
input handled, and no way out but the power switch. After a restart the
same video ended as it should.

The flip is now waited for 2 s. When it has not come by then:
- the commit of the frame waits. nvidia-drm takes a commit that does not
  wait only once the flip before it has come; one that waits is applied
  at once, and the flips that have not come are completed after 3 s
  ("Flip event timeout on head"), which signals their fences, the ones
  the GPU waits for too;
- when flips still do not come (a second stall within 10 s), the output
  is turned off and on again, as when the screen is blanked and woken.
  The output off first: that commit completes the flips, and frees the
  GPU, before the display resources are told that the display is lost,
  as for a change of mode (a renderer flushed for it waits for the GPU);
  if the driver does not take the output off, they are not told. If it
  refuses to turn the output on, it is asked again with the commit of a
  frame, after 1 s and then less often, up to once a minute, until it
  takes it. At most once a minute, and three times while Kodi runs;
  after that each stall costs about 5 s, and Kodi still handles input.
The fence of the frame goes to its own commit after all this, and a
fence that was given up on (or whose wait failed) is destroyed only once
it has signalled.

An output that is off on purpose has no flip to wait for and is left
alone. A flip comes within tens of milliseconds: nothing changes while
flips come. With the drivers that use the kernel's helpers (amdgpu,
i915), a commit that waits first waits for the GPU to finish the frame,
which waits for the flip before it: there a flip that never comes still
stops Kodi, in the commit instead of here.
---
 xbmc/utils/EGLFence.cpp                       |  41 +++++
 xbmc/utils/EGLFence.h                         |  13 ++
 .../windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp | 141 +++++++++++++++++-
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.h  |  23 +++
 4 files changed, 215 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/utils/EGLFence.cpp b/xbmc/utils/EGLFence.cpp
index d7bfb78..879b798 100644
--- a/xbmc/utils/EGLFence.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/utils/EGLFence.cpp
@@ -142,4 +142,45 @@ void CEGLFence::WaitSyncCPU()
     m_kmsFence = EGL_NO_SYNC_KHR;
   }
 }
+
+bool CEGLFence::WaitSyncCPU(std::chrono::milliseconds timeout)
+{
+  // The ones given up before, once they have signalled (or, past eight, the oldest: EGL deletes a
+  // sync object that a command still waits on only when that wait is over, EGL_KHR_wait_sync)
+  std::erase_if(m_givenUpKmsFences,
+                [this](EGLSyncKHR sync)
+                {
+                  EGLint status = EGL_UNSIGNALED_KHR;
+                  if (m_eglGetSyncAttribKHR(m_display, sync, EGL_SYNC_STATUS_KHR, &status) ==
+                          EGL_TRUE &&
+                      status != EGL_SIGNALED_KHR)
+                    return false;
+                  m_eglDestroySyncKHR(m_display, sync);
+                  return true;
+                });
+  if (m_givenUpKmsFences.size() > 8)
+  {
+    m_eglDestroySyncKHR(m_display, m_givenUpKmsFences.front());
+    m_givenUpKmsFences.erase(m_givenUpKmsFences.begin());
+  }
+
+  if (!m_kmsFence)
+    return true;
+
+  const EGLint result =
+      m_eglClientWaitSyncKHR(m_display, m_kmsFence, 0,
+                             static_cast<EGLTimeKHR>(timeout.count()) * 1000000ULL);
+  if (result == EGL_FALSE || result == EGL_TIMEOUT_EXPIRED_KHR)
+  {
+    // The GPU can still wait for it (WaitSyncGPU()): kept until it has signalled. The next frame
+    // brings the fence of its own commit. (A wait that failed left it to the next frame, which
+    // made a new one over it without destroying it.)
+    m_givenUpKmsFences.push_back(m_kmsFence);
+    m_kmsFence = EGL_NO_SYNC_KHR;
+    return result == EGL_FALSE;
+  }
+  m_eglDestroySyncKHR(m_display, m_kmsFence);
+  m_kmsFence = EGL_NO_SYNC_KHR;
+  return true;
+}
 #endif
